{"context":"The Pipeline Execution Model (ADR-007) defines how work is orchestrated — what runs, in what order, and under what conditions. But orchestration is not execution. Orchestration decides; execution performs.\n\nWithout a clear separation between orchestration and execution, the following problems emerge:\n\n**Orchestration coupled to process mechanics.** Pipeline logic becomes mixed with process spawning details — how to set environment variables, how to handle timeouts, how to capture output. A change in execution mechanics requires changes to orchestration code, and vice versa.\n\n**Duplicated execution logic.** Every subsystem that needs to run an external command implements its own process execution logic. Timeout handling, output capture, and error reporting are implemented inconsistently acr Source: ADR-008-process-runner-architecture.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil adopts a dedicated Process Runner architecture.\n\nThe Process Runner is the single architectural component responsible for launching and managing external processes.\n\nThe relationship between Pipeline and Process Runner is:\n\n- **Pipeline** orchestrates — decides what to run, in what order, and under what conditions\n- **Process Runner** executes — launches the process, monitors it, captures its output, and reports the result\n\nNo other subsystem MAY directly execute external commands. All external process execution MUST go through the Process Runner.\n\nThe Process Runner is a Core service. It is available to all architectural domains that need to execute external processes, but it is always invoked within the context of a Pipeline or a Pipeline-orchestrated operation.","consequences":"### 11.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Consistent execution behavior.** Every external process is executed through the same abstraction. Timeout handling, output capture, and error reporting are uniform across all subsystems.\n\n**Platform abstraction.** The Process Runner shields 